      "narrative": "The copper lamps gutter as you slide onto the bench across from Mara Venn. She's been watching you since you walked in \u2014 her sharp eyes miss little. She sets down her clay cup and folds her hands on the scarred tabletop.\n\n**\"The Ash Gate.\"** She says the name like it leaves a taste. **\"It's a crack in the world, that gate. Old Bram was the only one who knew how to work the lock \u2014 the real lock, not the iron one Toran fusses over. Bram carried people through for twenty years. Then, three nights ago, he didn't come back.\"**\n\nShe pauses, letting a pair of patrons shuffle past before leaning in.\n\n**\"They found him at dawn. Face-down on the landing, his own boat hook buried in his chest. The Grey Hind was still tied up, half-full of river-mist that never cleared. The town council sealed the gate that same morning. Too many questions, no answers, and a corpse that told no tales.\"**\n\nMara's eyes hold yours. **\"That's the official story. The one the Kestrel Guild signed off on. But I've been poking around since before the body went cold, and I'll tell you this \u2014 there's a reason Toran Vex is still in this tavern instead of at his post. He's the one who kept the gate's lock for years. And he's been sweating through his collar ever since Bram died.\"**\n\nShe nods subtly toward the bar, where the ink-stained clerk nurses a half-empty glass and doesn't meet anyone's gaze.\n\n**\"So that's what I know, ranger. The gate's closed because someone killed the only man who could open it. The question is \u2014 who wanted it shut that badly?\"**\n\nShe tilts her head, waiting for your next move."
    },
