      "narrative": "The tavern door swings shut behind you, cutting off the murmur of low voices and the clink of glasses. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp and cold, carrying the iron scent of wet stone and the distant whisper of moving water.\n\nThe street is narrow here, cobbles slick with last night's rain. The Copper Finch squats at the edge of town, and beyond it the buildings thin to scattered cottages with shuttered windows. A single track of packed earth leads north, curving between gnarled alders whose roots clutch at the mud like fingers.\n\nYou follow it for perhaps a quarter mile. The trees fall away. The ground opens onto a flat expanse of grey gravel, and there \u2014 standing alone against a pale sky \u2014 is the Ash Gate.\n\nIt's not what you expected. No grand archway, no fortified portal. Just a cracked black arch of stone, maybe twelve feet high and eight wide, set into a low ridge of rock that rises from the gravel like a whale's back. The stone is dark with moisture, veined with pale lichen. Beyond the arch, through its open throat, you can see only a wall of grey mist that does not stir or thin \u2014 it hangs there, dense as wool, swallowing all sight of what lies beyond.\n\nThe Grey Hind sits at a small wooden landing to the left of the arch, half-drawn up onto the gravel. A flat-bottomed ferry boat, weathered and dark, its hull beaded with moisture. A coil of rope lies loose on the deck. No body. No blood. Just the boat, waiting.\n\nThe ground around the gate is churned with boot prints \u2014 some fresh, some days old, layered over each other in the gravel. A length of rusted chain is looped through the iron ring on the left side of the arch, padlocked to a matching ring on the right. The lock is heavy and ornate, its keyhole crusted with verdigris.\n\nThe mist beyond the arch does not move. The silence here is deeper than it should be \u2014 the wind doesn't carry, the gravel doesn't crunch under your own feet the way it ought to.\n\nThe Ash Gate watches. Waits."
    },
