      "narrative": "Your voice carries across the gravel flat, swallowed by the heavy air.\n\n**\"Old Bram!\"**\n\nThe name echoes once off the ridge stone, then dies. No answer. The mist beyond the gate does not ripple. The Grey Hind's mooring rope creaks as the boat shifts against the landing.\n\nSilence settles back in \u2014 thicker than before, if anything. The kind of silence that has weight, that presses against the ears. Somewhere behind you, a bird calls once and falls quiet.\n\nThe padlock on the gate's iron rings glints dully, verdigris crusting its seams like old scabs. The chain it secures is rusted, but intact \u2014 no sign of forced entry, no dropped tools, no evidence anyone has touched it in days.\n\nThe Grey Hind's hull ticks softly as moisture expands in the wood. A single footprint \u2014 half-washed by rain \u2014 mars the gravel near the boat's bow, but it's too smudged to read.\n\nNothing stirs. Nothing answers.\n\nThe gate waits, patient as a toothless mouth."
