      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch at the threshold of the arch, scanning the gravel inch by inch. Your fingers brush the damp stones as you trace the line where the ridge meets the packed earth, looking for anything \u2014 a scrap of cloth, a dropped coin, a scuff mark that tells a story.\n\nNothing.\n\nThe gravel lies undisturbed, each stone settled in its bed as if combed into place by wind and rain. The moss that clings to the base of the arch grows in an even carpet \u2014 no broken patches, no crushed stems, no sign that anyone has knelt or stood or struggled here in weeks. The dark soil beneath is uniformly damp, unmarked by any impression deeper than a passing raindrop.\n\nYou sit back on your heels. The gate looms above you, silent. Whatever story this place holds, the ground isn't giving it up.\n\nThe padlock clinks softly as the chain shifts in a breeze you can't feel on your skin."
