      "narrative": "The walk back passes under a sky that's brightening reluctantly, grey clouds thinning to reveal a pale wash of sun that does little to warm the air. By the time you push through the Copper Finch's door, the morning crowd has thinned \u2014 just two figures hunched over a corner table nursing cups of something dark, and the barman wiping a glass behind the counter.\n\nYour old table sits empty by the window, the scarred wood catching a sliver of grey light. The chair scrapes as you pull it out. The seat is still warm from someone else's body \u2014 or perhaps that's just the residual heat of the hearth.\n\nThe barman glances up. Middle-aged, heavy-shouldered, with the look of a man who's been standing in the same room for twenty years. He sets down the glass and walks over, wiping his hands on a rag tucked into his apron.\n\n**\"Back already.\"** Not a question. His voice is rough, scraped by years of smoke and small talk. **\"Find what you were looking for out there?\"**\n\nHe stops at your table, rag in hand, waiting. His eyes are flat, curious without being friendly \u2014 the look of a man who's seen too many strangers pass through asking too many questions."
